WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:01.870
- [Sal] In a previous video,

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:06.610
we talk about the notion of
equivalence with equations.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.300
And equivalence is just this notion

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.930
that there's different ways of writing

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.420
what our equivalent statements in algebra.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.740
And I can give some simple examples.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.880
I could say two x equals 10,
or I could say x equals five.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.910
These are equivalent equations.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:22.910
Why are they?

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.010
Because an x satisfies one of them

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.160
if and only if it satisfies the other.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.205
And you can verify that in both cases,

00:00:29.205 --> 00:00:33.140
x equals five is the only
x that satisfies both.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.732
Another set of equivalent
equations, you could have two x

00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:40.350
is equal to eight and x equals four.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.260
These two are equivalent equations.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.820
An x satisfies one if and only
if it satisfies the other.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.050
In this video, we're going
to extend our knowledge

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.520
of equivalence to thinking
about equivalent systems.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.463
And really, in your past
when you were solving

00:00:54.463 --> 00:00:57.415
systems of equations,
you were doing operations

00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:59.560
assuming equivalence, but you might not

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.990
have just been thinking about it that way.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.170
So let's give ourselves a system.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.680
So let's say this system tells us

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.860
that there's some x y pair
where two times that x

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.130
plus that y is equal to eight,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.130
and that x plus that y is equal to five.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.400
Now we can have an equivalent system

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.060
if we replace either of these equations

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.760
with an equivalent version.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.830
So for example, many of you when you look

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.050
to try to solve this, you might say well,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.080
if this was a negative two x here,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.310
maybe I could eventually
add the left side.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.960
And we'll talk about why that is

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.870
an equivalence preserving operation.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.180
But in order to get a
negative two out here,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.570
you'd have to multiply
this entire equation

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:45.950
times negative two.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.837
And so if you did that, if you
multiplied both sides of this

00:01:48.837 --> 00:01:52.820
times negative two, times negative two,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.040
what you're going to get is negative two x

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.220
minus two y is equal to negative 10.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.940
This equation and this
equation are equivalent.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:04.773
Why?

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:07.230
Because any x y pair that
satisfies one of them

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.110
will satisfy the other, or
an x y pair satisfies one

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.050
if and only if it satisfies the other.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.470
And so if I now think about the system,

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.140
the system where I've
rewritten this second equation

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.864
and my first equation is the same,

00:02:20.864 --> 00:02:25.864
this is an equivalent
system to our first system.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.210
So these, any x y pair, if an x y pair

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.930
satisfies one of these systems,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.193
it's going to satisfy
the other and vice versa.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.110
Now the next interesting
thing that you might realize,

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.840
and if you were just trying to solve this,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.140
and this isn't an introductory
video in solving systems,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.280
so I'm assuming some familiarity with it,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.870
you've probably seen
solving by elimination

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.865
where you say okay, look,
if I can somehow add these,

00:02:49.865 --> 00:02:52.030
the left side to the left
side and the right side

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.090
to the right side, these
x's will quote cancel out

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.670
and then I'll just be left with y's.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.010
And we've done this before.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.440
You can kind of think you're
trying to solve for y.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.110
But in this video, I
want to think about why

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.268
you end up with an equivalent system

00:03:04.268 --> 00:03:06.140
if you were to do that.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.350
And one way to think about it is

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.300
what I'm going to do to create
an equivalent system here

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.800
is I am going to keep my first equation,

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.078
two x plus y is equal to eight.

00:03:19.078 --> 00:03:22.040
But then I'm gonna take my second equation

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.780
and add the same thing to both sides.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.640
We know if you add or
subtract the same thing

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.050
to both sides of an equation,

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.290
you get an equivalent equation.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.740
So I'm gonna do that over here.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.350
But it's gonna be a
little bit interesting.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.130
So if you had negative two x minus two y

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.740
is equal to negative 10,
and what I want to do

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.890
is I want to add eight to both sides.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.456
So I could do it like this.

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:46.640
I could add eight to both sides.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.970
But remember, our system
is saying that both

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:50.296
of these statements are true,

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:53.410
that two x plus y is equal to eight

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:56.960
and negative two x minus two
y is equal to negative 10.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.460
So instead of adding
explicitly eight to both sides,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.802
I could add something that's equivalent

00:04:01.802 --> 00:04:04.060
to eight to both sides.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.313
And I know something that
is equivalent to eight

00:04:06.313 --> 00:04:08.830
based on this first equation.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.200
I could add eight, and I could do eight

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.840
on the left hand side, or I
could just add two x plus y.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.945
So two x plus y.

00:04:16.945 --> 00:04:19.440
Now I really want you, you
might want to pause your video

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.308
and say okay, how can I do this?

00:04:21.308 --> 00:04:23.360
Why is Sal saying that I'm adding

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.300
the same thing to both sides?

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.500
Because remember, when
we're taking a system,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.150
we're assuming that both
of these need to be true.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.071
An x y pair satisfies one
equation if and only if,

00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:36.310
only if, if and only if
it satisfies the other.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.700
So here, we know that x, two x plus y

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.520
needs to be equal to eight.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.198
So if I'm adding two x plus y to the left

00:04:43.198 --> 00:04:44.890
and I'm adding eight to the right,

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.150
I'm really just adding
eight to both sides,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.960
which is equivalence preserving.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.680
And when you do that, you
get, these negative two x

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.290
and two x cancels out, you get negative y

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.280
is equal to negative two.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.810
And so I can rewrite that second equation

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.707
as negative y is equal to negative two.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:09.070
And I know what you're thinking.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.410
You're like wait, but I'm used

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.300
to solving systems of equations.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.080
I'm used to just adding these two together

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.670
and then I just have this one equation.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:19.400
And really, that's not super
mathematically rigorous

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.780
because the other equation is still there.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.910
It's still a constraint.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.260
Oftentimes, you solve for one

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.600
and then you quote substitute back in.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.377
But really, the both equations
are there the whole time.

00:05:29.377 --> 00:05:32.850
You're just rewriting
them in equivalent ways.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.930
So once again, this system, this system,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.540
and this system are all equivalent.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.965
Any x y pair that
satisfies one will satisfy

00:05:38.965 --> 00:05:41.540
all of them, and vice versa.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.100
And once again, we can continue

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.610
to rewrite this in equivalent ways.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.580
That second equation, I can multiply

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.960
both sides by negative one.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.550
That's equivalence preserving.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.660
And if I did that, then
I get, I haven't changed

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.380
my top equation, two x
plus y is equal to eight.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.620
And on the second one,
if I multiply both sides

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.380
by negative one, I get y is equal to two.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.020
Once again, these are
all equivalent systems.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.900
I know I'm, I sound
very repetitive in this.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.300
But now, I can do another
thing to make this,

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.100
to keep the equivalence
but get a clearer idea

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.270
of what that x y pair is.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.510
If we know that y is equal to two

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.310
and we know that that's
true in both equations,

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.790
remember, it is an and here.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.890
We're assuming there's x y, some x y pair

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.600
that satisfies both.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.580
Two x plus y needs to be equal to eight

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.040
and y is equal to two.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.550
Well that means up here where we see a y,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.470
we can write an equivalent system

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:39.610
where instead of writing a y there,

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.370
we could write a two because
we know that y is equal to two.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.420
And so we can rewrite that top equation

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.330
by substituting a two for y.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:53.330
So we could rewrite that as two
x plus two is equal to eight

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:57.200
and y is equal to two.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.320
So this is an and right over there.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.330
It's implicitly there.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.600
And of course, we can
keep going from there.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.400
I'll scroll down a little bit.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.340
I could write another
equivalent system to this

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.730
by doing equivalence preserving operations

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:11.740
on that top equation.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:14.040
What if I subtracted two from both sides

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:14.930
of that top equation?

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:17.200
It's still going to be
an equivalent equation.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.300
And so I could rewrite
it as, if I subtract two

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.690
from both sides, I'm gonna
get two x is equal to six.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.970
And then that second
equation hasn't changed.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.090
Y is equal to two.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.860
So there's some x y pair
that if it satisfies one,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.240
it satisfies the other, and vice versa.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.570
This system is equivalent to every system

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.020
that I've written so far in
this chain of operations,

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.750
so to speak, and then of
course, this top equation,

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.270
an equivalence preserving operation

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.640
is to divide both sides by a non,

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.090
the same nonzero value.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.540
And in this case, I could
divide both sides by two.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:53.280
And then I would get, if
I divide the top by two,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.280
I would get x equals three,
and y is equal to two.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.260
And once again, this is a different way

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:01.093
of thinking about it.

00:08:01.093 --> 00:08:04.680
All I'm doing is rewriting the same system

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.190
in an equivalent way that just
gets us a little bit clearer

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:12.580
as to what that x y pair actually is.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:14.740
In the past, you might've just, you know,

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.200
just assumed that you can
add both sides of an equation

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:18.630
or do this type of elimination

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.080
or do some type of substitution

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.047
to just quote figure out the x and y.

00:08:22.047 --> 00:08:24.850
But really, you're rewriting the system.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:26.600
You're rewriting the
constraints of the system

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:29.570
in equivalent ways to
make it more explicit

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.000
what that x y pair is that satisfies

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.883
both equations in the system.

